            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Rousseau</strong><br>\nJean-Jacques Rousseau was born in Geneva in 1712. His mother died a few days after his birth, and he was raised by his father, a watchmaker. When he was ten years old, his father had to leave Geneva following a dispute, and Rousseau was entrusted to a pastor for upbringing. Later, he worked as an apprentice for a notary and an engraver, but had negative experiences with both.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Education and Influences</strong><br>\nRousseau's education was informal and largely self-taught. He read numerous books and was particularly influenced by classical literature and the works of Plutarch. At the age of 16, he left Geneva and traveled to France and Italy, where he encountered various patrons who encouraged his studies. These travels shaped his views on society and human nature.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Beginning of His Writing Career</strong><br>\nRousseau began writing in the 1740s. His first major success was the \"Discourse on the Sciences and Arts,\" in which he argued that the development of arts and sciences had led to the corruption of virtue and morality. This work gained him recognition in philosophical circles and established his reputation as a thinker challenging conventional wisdom.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "fr"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
