          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Important Works</strong><br>\nOne of Rousseau's most significant works is \"The Social Contract,\" in which he expounded his theories on freedom and equality. He argued that government resulted from a social contract among citizens and its legitimacy depended on the consent of the governed. His influence extended to political reforms and revolutions, including the French Revolution.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Exile</strong><br>\nRousseau's personal life was marked by conflicts and controversies. Due to his ideas, he often clashed with other thinkers and had to leave France. He spent some time in exile in Switzerland and Britain, where he continued to write and develop his theories despite growing paranoia and a sense of persecution.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Return to France</strong><br>\nAfter several years in exile, Rousseau returned to France under an assumed name. He lived modestly, continuing to write and revise earlier works. However, his health deteriorated, and he experienced periods of illness and depression, all while continuing to combat criticism of his philosophical opinions.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "fr"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
