          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Nature and Man</strong><br>\nRousseau is known for his reflections on nature and \"natural man.\" In works like \"Emile or On Education,\" he argued that humans are naturally good and that society corrupts them. He emphasized the importance of early education and believed that a life close to nature was the key to virtue and happiness.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence on Pedagogy and Education</strong><br>\nHis ideas on education and childhood had a lasting impact on pedagogical theory. Rousseau believed that education should cater to the child's needs and that learning should occur through experience. His novel \"Emile\" describes the fictional education of a boy, representing a radical innovation in thinking about education.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Posterity</strong><br>\nRousseau died in 1778, but his legacy endured. His ideas influenced the Enlightenment and modern political theories. He has often been considered one of the early thinkers who paved the way for democratic movements in Europe. Today, his writings are still studied and discussed, and his reflections on freedom and the social contract remain relevant.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "fr"
